CELLS = {
    0: '# Notebook 05 — From Raw Diffractogram to Chemically Interpretable Numerical Descriptors\n\nA diffractogram is a **continuous curve**. A curve cannot be directly sorted, filtered, correlated, or fed into a machine learning model. A query like *"show me syntheses with large unit cells and low crystallinity"* cannot be answered by a relational database as long as information resides in graphical curve formats.\n\nThis notebook builds that bridge: transforming each raw diffractogram into **a row of numerical descriptors**, justifying every feature against real laboratory experimental data.\n\n### How to Read This Notebook\n\n- **Zero raw disk file access.** All data is queried from PostgreSQL via `SELECT`. The database is the single source of truth; raw instrument files move across local folders and lack reproducibility.\n- **All computational logic resides in `src/pxrd_descriptors.py`.** The notebook imports this module rather than reimplementing algorithms, guaranteeing that the 1,800-measurement batch pipeline and these interactive figures execute identical code without discrepancy.\n- **Markdown prose is translated, while figure elements remain in English.** Visualizations are publication-ready for manuscripts and web applications.\n- **Every decision is quantitatively measured rather than heuristically assumed.** Angular cutoffs, λ smoothness parameters, and detection thresholds are accompanied by empirical benchmark validations.\n\n### Table of Contents\n\n| # | Section | Technical Problem Addressed |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Connection & Utilities | Database client and shared plotting scaffold |\n| 2 | Database Inventory | Measurement classes and reference standards |\n| 3 | Signal Processing Rationale | Reconciling angular grid and intensity scale discrepancies |\n| 4 | **Low-Angle Cutoff Calibration** | The 5° 2θ boundary: removing direct beam scatter |\n| 5 | Three-Component Signal Anatomy | Separating instrumental background, amorphous halo, and Bragg peaks |\n| 6 | Baseline Parameter λ Calibration | Quantitative tuning against blank and standard references |\n| 7 | Amorphous Halo vs. Peak Separation | Width filtering failure vs. physical blank subtraction |\n| 8 | Poisson-Calibrated Peak Detection | Bonferroni-corrected variable false-positive thresholding |\n| 9 | Difficult Case Studies | Resolving weak Bragg peaks over dominant amorphous halos |\n| 10 | **Comprehensive Descriptor Vector** | Mathematical definitions and code derivations |\n| 11 | Reference Standard Validation | Validating pipeline integrity against known controls |\n| 12 | Database Ingestion into `pxrd_descriptors` | Batch UPSERT execution |\n| 13 | Exploratory Corpus Analysis | Corpus-wide descriptor distributions and correlations |\n| 14 | Methodological Best Practices | Reusable engineering principles for analytical spectroscopy |\n',
    1: '# 1. Connection & Utilities\n\nThree primary components: `sql()` for executing database queries, `cargar_curva()` for retrieving normalized diffractograms mapped to the canonical angular grid, and shared aesthetic plotting utilities.\n\n`cargar_curva()` implements an important safeguard: grid points falling **outside the measured instrument angular range are discarded**, never interpolated. Interpolating beyond boundaries would invent synthetic counts at unmeasured angles. Preserving the distinction between "zero signal" and "unmeasured region" is mandatory across all downstream processing.\n',
    3: '# 2. Database Inventory\n\nBefore processing, we establish available dataset scope across two dimensions: **measurement volume** and **sample typology**. The distinction between analytical samples and reference standards is essential: reference scans serve as the **ground-truth benchmarks** against which algorithms are calibrated.\n',
    5: 'The four sample families and their analytical roles:\n\n| `measurement_tag` | Classification | Analytical Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `as_synthesized` | Laboratory synthesis samples | Target experimental corpus to describe |\n| `reference_blank` | **Empty sample holder** | Quantifies instrument response in the absence of sample ("zero" baseline) |\n| `reference_standard` | Certified instrument standard | Determines instrumental resolution: theoretical minimum peak FWHM |\n| `reference_precursor` | Metal salts and uncoordinated organic linkers | Starting materials lacking MOF framework topology: distinguishes reagent signatures |\n| `simulated_scxrd` | Calculated patterns from solved single-crystal structures | Ground-truth reference for novel phase triage |\n\n> **Why blank and certified standard scans are indispensable.** Signal processing involves free hyperparameters. Selecting parameters based on external literature defaults is subjective. Calibrating parameters against samples with **known ground-truth responses** — an empty holder exhibits zero diffraction; a certified standard exhibits known peak broadening — constitutes rigorous calibration. Section 6 relies entirely on the existence of these reference scans.\n',
    6: '# 3. Signal Processing Rationale\n\nTwo raw diffractograms of the identical chemical phase can be numerically incomparable due to non-chemical acquisition discrepancies across two distinct dimensions: angular grid spacing and intensity scaling.\n\n## 3.1 Angular Grid Incompatibility\n',
    9: '**Observations.** Three histograms illustrate acquisition discrepancies across scans: scan start angle, scan termination angle, and step size. None are constant. Logarithmic scaling is applied to (a) and (c) to reveal minority acquisition configurations that would otherwise remain hidden on linear axes.\n\n**The requirement for a canonical grid.** Subtracting two curves point-by-point requires point *i* on scan A and point *i* on scan B to map to the identical Bragg angle. Variable steps (e.g., 0.0131° vs. 0.0263°) prevent direct vector operations. We resample all scans onto a **canonical grid** with a step size of 0.02° — narrower than the minimum instrumental resolution — and **mask each scan to its measured range**.\n\n> **Statistical considerations.** Linear interpolation between measured adjacent points is a valid continuous approximation *within* the measured boundary. Extrapolating *outside* the boundary is invalid because no data constrains the signal. Therefore, `resample_to_grid` returns a boolean validity mask rather than zero-filling unmeasured angular regions.\n',
    10: '## 3.2 Intensity Scale Incompatibility\n\nA fast screening scan and a high-count Rietveld refinement scan of the **identical material** can differ in total integrated counts by a factor of 30.\n',
    12: '**Observations.** In (a), the low-count scan (red) appears flat purely due to the y-axis scale of the high-count scan (blue, 33x higher intensity). In (b), normalizing each scan by its integrated area yields complete profile overlap. **Profile geometry contains structural information; absolute count magnitude reflects detector acquisition dwell time.**\n\n> **Why area normalization must precede baseline estimation.** The arPLS baseline algorithm (Section 6) utilizes a smoothness hyperparameter, λ, that controls baseline stiffness **relative to the numerical magnitude of the input data**. Applied to raw counts, a fixed λ acts as an excessively stiff baseline on a 200-count scan and an excessively flexible baseline on a 20,000-count scan. Prior area normalization ensures that a single λ parameter behaves identically across all 1,883 scans.\n',
    13: '# 4. Low-Angle Cutoff Calibration: Defining the Measurement Window\n\nThis section determines the lower angular boundary of the canonical grid — a single parameter that shifts crystallinity metrics across the **entire corpus**.\n\n## 4.1 Physical Mechanism\n\nAt very low scattering angles, the detector aligns closely with the incident X-ray beam path. Measured intensity in this regime is dominated by **direct beam penetration and parasitic air/slit scattering** rather than coherent Bragg diffraction from sample crystallites. This generates a steep, monotonically decaying low-angle background tail present in **all measurements**, including empty sample holders.\n\nIf included in processing, baseline and halo estimation algorithms mistake this parasitic tail for a massive low-angle amorphous halo, corrupting crystallinity calculations and hallucinating large unit-cell parameters.\n\nThe question is not *whether* to truncate low angles, but **where**. Three independent empirical validations determine this threshold.\n',
    14: '## 4.2 Validation 1: Median Background Across 200 Random Syntheses\n\nIf a low-angle intensity rise persists across the **median profile of 200 chemically distinct materials**, it cannot represent coherent Bragg diffraction: 200 diverse materials do not share an identical low-angle reflection. The signature is strictly instrumental.\n\nEach scan is normalized by its mean intensity in the high-angle background window (20–45° 2θ) to compare geometric profile shape independently of absolute count magnitude.\n',
    17: '**Observations.** In (a), the median profile of 200 samples reaches **3.6 times** background intensity at 3.0° 2θ, decaying monotonically to 1.3x at **4.4° 2θ**. The interquartile range demonstrates that this parasitic tail is ubiquitous. In (b), **11 starting material precursors** (metal salts and small organic ligands lacking MOF-scale unit cells) exhibit the identical parasitic tail (3.7x to 15.5x at 3.0–3.5° 2θ), returning to baseline (~1.0x) around 4.5–5.0° 2θ.\n\n**Validation 3: Reference Scan Alignment.** Both certified instrument standards and empty holder blank scans were acquired starting at **5.0066° 2θ**. Processing angles below 5.0° lacks reference baseline data.\n\n**Design Decision: The canonical grid begins at 5.0° 2θ**, the lowest angle that eliminates parasitic direct-beam scatter while aligning with experimental reference standards.\n',
    18: "## 4.3 Analytical Implications of the 5.0° 2θ Cutoff\n\nApplying Bragg's law, $d = \\lambda / (2\\sin\\theta)$, converts the angular cutoff into a **maximum resolvable interplanar spacing**: at 5.0° 2θ with Cu Kα₁ radiation, the maximum observable d-spacing is d = 17.66 Å.\n\nLarger d-spacings exist in MOF chemistry, but **fall outside this instrument's optical geometry**. To ensure data integrity, `pxrd_descriptors` stores a `d_max_saturated` boolean flag marking scans whose lowest-angle reflection coincides with the 5.0° cutoff, indicating that `d_max_angstrom` represents a **lower bound** rather than a fully resolved reflection.\n",
    20: '> **Note on the large unit cell diagnostic window (15° vs. 10° 2θ).** Phosphonate MOFs (P-MOFs) are dense frameworks characterized by bulky aromatic linker cores, yielding large, low-symmetry unit cells. Their primary diagnostic Bragg reflections typically appear **between 10° and 15° 2θ**; restricting the window to 10° 2θ truncates half of the diagnostic regime.\n>\n> A technical clarification: intense low-angle reflections indicate a **large unit cell**, suggesting **potential porosity**. In phosphonate MOFs, large unit cells stem from ligand molecular dimensions and do not guarantee open pore channels. True accessible porosity must be confirmed via gas adsorption physisorption isotherms, not powder diffraction alone.\n',
    21: '# 5. Diffractogram Signal Anatomy: Three Superimposed Contributions\n\nAn experimental powder pattern comprises **three distinct physical contributions**, each requiring separate mathematical treatment:\n\n| Component | Physical Origin | Processing Protocol |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Instrumental Background** | Air scattering, sample substrate, detector thermal noise | **Subtracted.** Carries no chemical information about the sample |\n| **Amorphous Halo** | Incoherent diffuse scattering from disordered solid fraction | **Quantified.** Directly measures the non-crystalline solid fraction |\n| **Bragg Reflections** | Coherent constructive interference from crystalline lattice planes | **Quantified.** Measures the crystalline phase |\n\nTreating instrumental background and amorphous scattering as a single monolithic "background" destroys quantitative crystallinity calculation — where the amorphous halo *is* the core metric — and risks subtracting weak Bragg peaks residing atop diffuse halos.\n',
    23: '**Observations.** Top panel: raw measurement (gray) and instrumental background contribution (brown). Bottom panel: background-subtracted signal decomposed into broad diffuse amorphous scattering (orange) and narrow crystalline Bragg reflections (blue).\n\nCrystallinity index is rigorously formulated over **integrated profile areas**, not peak heights:\n\n$$X_c = \\frac{A_{\\text{crystalline}}}{A_{\\text{crystalline}} + A_{\\text{amorphous}}}$$\n\n> **Why integrated areas are superior to peak heights.** Measuring peak heights above zero treats the underlying diffuse halo as crystalline signal, introducing positive bias that **scales with amorphous content** — disproportionately distorting marginal, poorly crystalline samples. Area integration assigns each scattered photon to exactly one physical phase.\n',
    24: '# 6. Baseline Parameter λ Calibration\n\n## 6.1 Asymmetrically Reweighted Penalized Least Squares (arPLS)\n\n**arPLS** (Baek et al., *Analyst* 140 (2015) 250) fits a smooth baseline to raw data using penalized least squares with asymmetric iteratively reweighted residuals. The intuitive analog is an elastic spline pushed from beneath the curve: **λ governs spline stiffness**.\n\n- **Small λ** → overly flexible spline: tracks broad peaks, absorbing and erasing the amorphous halo upon subtraction.\n- **Large λ** → excessively rigid spline: fails to follow genuine instrumental curvature, leaving residual background.\n\nThe asymmetric weighting suppresses points lying **above** the fitted baseline (Bragg peaks) across iterations using a generalized logistic function:\n\n$$w_i = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp\\!\\left(\\dfrac{2\\,(d_i - (2\\sigma^- - \\mu^-))}{\\sigma^-}\\right)}$$\n\nwhere $\\mu^-$ and $\\sigma^-$ denote the mean and standard deviation of **negative residuals** (data points falling below the baseline estimate). Restricting statistics to negative residuals isolates background noise free from peak contamination, enabling arPLS to track the physical noise floor.\n\n## 6.2 Quantitative Calibration Protocol\n\n| Reference Sample | Evaluation Criterion | Failure Mode |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Empty Holder Blank** | Residual background post-subtraction ≈ 0 | Excessively **high** λ: incomplete background removal |\n| **Amorphous Synthesis** | Diffuse halo preserved without attenuation | Excessively **low** λ: baseline absorbs amorphous halo |\n| **Certified Standard** | Bragg reflection FWHM preserved without distortion | Baseline distorts peak broadening metrics |\n',
    28: '**Observations.** Reading multi-objective criteria across parameter sweeps:\n\n- **(a)** Blank residual area rises sharply for λ > 10⁷: baseline stiffness prevents tracking background curvature.\n- **(b)** Amorphous halo area collapses for λ < 10⁴: the baseline absorbs diffuse scattering.\n- **(c)** Certified standard peak FWHM remains invariant across wide λ ranges, serving as a **negative control** verifying peak geometry stability.\n\n**λ = 10⁶** represents the optimal inflection point: the maximum stiffness preserving complete background subtraction before residual growth begins.\n\n> `pxrd_descriptors` stores `baseline_lambda` across **every database record** to ensure full algorithmic provenance if parameters are updated in future revisions.\n',
    29: '# 7. Amorphous Halo Separation: Comparative Evaluation of Three Methods\n\n## 7.0 Mathematical Definitions\n\nLet normalized measurement be $I(2\\theta)$, fitted arPLS baseline be $B(2\\theta)$, and net signal be $N = I - B$. The total baseline $B$ represents the sum of two physical components:\n\n$$B(2\\theta) = \\underbrace{A(2\\theta)}_{\\text{sample diffuse scattering}} + \\underbrace{E(2\\theta)}_{\\text{instrumental background}}$$\n\nSeparating sample diffuse halo $A$ from instrument scatter $E$ is evaluated across **three candidate methods**:\n\n| Method | Conceptual Approach | Experimental Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| **7.1** Dual-pass arPLS | Secondary flexible arPLS on net signal $N$ | **Failed**: identically evaluates to zero |\n| **7.2** Width-selective filter | Rolling median window (3.0° 2θ) | **Failed**: overestimates halo beneath dense peak clusters |\n| **7.3** Blank Subtraction | $A = B - E$, using measured reference blank | **Validated & Implemented** |\n',
    30: '## 7.0.1 Reference Benchmark Scans\n\nEvaluated across three canonical samples representing median cases within their respective expert-labeled categories: amorphous, incipiently crystalline, and fully crystalline.\n',
    32: '## 7.1 Method 1: Dual-Pass arPLS\n\n**Hypothesis.** If a stiff arPLS pass extracts instrumental baseline $E$, a secondary flexible pass on net signal $N$ should extract the smooth diffuse halo $A$.\n\n**Failure Mechanism.** arPLS is an envelope-floor estimator. Net signal $N = I - B$ already has its baseline floor at zero by construction. Requesting the baseline floor of a zero-anchored signal returns **zero**.\n\nThe failure is **silent**: returning a flat zero curve without runtime errors, resulting in 0% calculated amorphous area and falsely classifying glass-like samples as 100% crystalline.\n',
    34: '## 7.2 Method 2: Width-Selective Rolling Median Filter\n\n**Hypothesis.** Bragg reflections are narrow (FWHM 0.1–0.3° 2θ), whereas amorphous halos are broad (5–15° 2θ). A rolling median filter with a 3.0° 2θ window should filter narrow peaks while retaining broad halos.\n\n**The Underlying Assumption.** A median filter ignores peaks only if peak points represent a **minority within the window**.\n',
    36: '**Failure Mechanism.** On fully **crystalline** samples exhibiting sharp reflections, Method 2 assigns ~50% of integrated intensity to amorphous halo, tracking the baseline contour beneath peak clusters.\n',
    38: '**Diagnosis.** Peak broadening and tails span ~50% of the angular axis in dense crystalline patterns. Within a 3.0° window centered on peak multiplets, elevated peak points become the majority, causing the rolling median to track peak tails rather than inter-peak baselines.\n\n> **Methodological Rule.** When an algorithmic filter relies on a distributional assumption (e.g., peak sparsity within a sliding window), that assumption must be quantitatively verified on real data.\n\n### Two Patches That Fail to Salvage Method 2\n\n| Patch | Proposed Fix | Measured Benchmark Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mask peaks and interpolate baseline beneath | Exclude peak tails from calculation | With the required wide mask (±0.5°), the **amorphous** reference collapses from 0.95 to 0.42, erasing genuine halos |\n| Lower percentile (median → 10th percentile) | Follow valleys rather than peak flanks | Corrects the crystalline benchmark (0.04) but reduces the amorphous benchmark to 0.15 |\n\nNeither satisfies the fundamental acceptance criterion: **a valid fix must resolve the failure case without degrading previously sound performance**. Method discarded.\n',
    39: '## 7.3 Method 3: Measured Instrumental Blank Subtraction\n\nInstead of inferring halo geometry from profile curvature, Method 3 directly measures and subtracts the instrumental background:\n\n$$B = A + E \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\boxed{A = B - E}$$\n\n### Empirical Justification\n\nThis method is physically valid only if the instrument contributes an **unstructured background**. If it contributed structured features, subtracting it would remove sample information. This is verified by measuring baseline curvature relative to a line connecting scan endpoints.\n',
    41: 'Blank sample holder baseline measurements are linear within statistical noise. **The instrument contributes zero baseline curvature**; hence, all curvature observed in an experimental sample originates from the sample itself — exactly what arPLS was discarding as background.\n\nThis also explains the failure of Method 1: in amorphous samples, arPLS **had already absorbed the diffuse halo** during the initial pass, because a 5–15° broad feature is smooth enough for a baseline estimator to treat as background. Hence, the second pass found nothing left to extract.\n\n> This mirrors the classic FTIR pitfall where baseline fitting inadvertently subtracts broad absorption bands. The algorithm performs as designed; the error lies in expecting it to distinguish two contributions without external physical reference.\n\n### Intensity Rescaling Factor\n\nBecause acquisition dwell times vary between blank references and experimental scans, and dwell time is unrecorded in reviewed `.xrdml` headers, raw counts are not directly subtractable. They are matched via scale factor $k$ fitted **above 45° 2θ**, where sample diffuse scattering has largely decayed:\n\n$$k = \\frac{\\mathrm{median}\\,[B(2\\theta > 45^\\circ)]}{\\mathrm{median}\\,[E(2\\theta > 45^\\circ)]}\n\\qquad\\qquad A = \\max(B - k\\,E,\\; 0)$$\n\nMedian calculation is used rather than the mean to prevent any residual high-angle Bragg reflections from skewing the scaling factor.\n\n### Blank Smoothing Prior to Subtraction\n\nThe empty holder blank is a low-count scan (median ~80 counts) with elevated relative Poisson noise. Scaling raw counts upward amplifies noise, degrading subtraction quality. Therefore, **$E$ is defined as the arPLS baseline of the blank**, preserving smooth baseline subtraction.\n\n### Quantitative Amorphous Fraction Definition\n\n$$A_{\\text{frac}} = \\frac{\\int A}{\\int A + \\int (I - B)}$$\n\nThis normalizes diffuse scattering strictly by total sample scattering, ensuring invariance to instrumental background level and exposure time.\n',
    43: '### Method 3 Output Across Reference Benchmarks\n\nIllustrating experimental profile $I$, arPLS baseline $B$, scaled instrumental background $kE$, and extracted amorphous halo area $A$ (orange).\n',
    45: '## 7.4 Validation Against 1,881 Expert Human Labels\n\nWe benchmark Method 2 vs. Method 3 against 1,881 visually labeled experimental scans classified into three categories independently of algorithmic descriptors.\n',
    48: 'Method 3 cleanly separates label distributions, whereas Method 2 compresses all categories toward high amorphous values due to peak tail artifacts.\n\n### Sub-Classification Benchmark: Incipient Crystallization Subtypes\n\nThe coarse three-class split can be achieved by simple peak counting. The rigorous test evaluates incipient subtypes distinguishing **three distinct intermediate crystallization regimes**:\n\n| Subtype | Structural Description | Expected Diffuse Fraction $A$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| `HALO_DOMINANTE` | Dominant diffuse halo with superimposed weak Bragg reflections | Highest |\n| `PICOS_ANCHOS` | Broadened Bragg peaks with moderate diffuse scattering | Intermediate |\n| `CRISTALITO_PEQUENO` | Small crystalline domains with minimal diffuse scattering | Lowest |\n',
    50: '## 7.5 Physical Scope and Measurement Limitations\n\nThe descriptor measures the **fraction of total scattering that is non-Bragg**. This includes:\n\n| Contribution | Physical Mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| Amorphous solid phase | Short-range atomic order, broad diffuse scattering |\n| Thermal & defect diffuse scattering | Inelastic phonon scattering, increases with scattering angle |\n| Sample substrate / capillary | Glass or PMMA holder scattering |\n| Air scattering | Optical path interaction |\n| X-ray fluorescence | Fe under Cu Kα radiation |\n\n> The descriptor quantifies **the non-Bragg scattering fraction of the diffractogram**, not the absolute gravimetric percentage of amorphous phase in the vial.\n\n### Silicon NIST 640 Standard Validation\n\nCertified NIST 640 silicon exhibits zero diffuse scattering; its residual low-angle intensity reflects mounting substrate interaction. Without an internal standard, PXRD crystallinity metrics provide **robust relative ranking within a standardized corpus**, not absolute gravimetric percentages.\n',
    52: 'Silicon has **no** Bragg reflections below 28° 2θ, where its small diffuse excess resides. This represents the sample **mounting substrate** rather than amorphous silicon. The subtraction assumes the sample was mounted identically to the blank. **Within a uniformly mounted corpus, comparative ranking is fully valid; across differing sample mounting configurations, caution is required.**\n',
    53: '### Methodological Detection Floor (~0.02)\n\nEvaluating synthetic patterns with known zero amorphous content demonstrates that arPLS baseline curvature slightly tracks peak tails, establishing an empirical **detection floor of ~0.02**. Calculated amorphous fractions below 0.02 reflect baseline artifact limits rather than measurable amorphous solid fractions.\n\n| Reflection FWHM | Peak Area Absorbed by Baseline |\n|---|---|\n| 0.10 – 1.00° | **0.1 – 0.3 %** |\n| 1.50° | 1.4 % |\n| 2.50° | **25 %** |\n\nAcross synthetic patterns **with zero amorphous phase**, the pipeline yields values between **0.006 and 0.018** rather than 0.0. In this corpus, median FWHM spans 0.16–0.24° (90th percentile < 0.57°), well within the safe operational regime (FWHM < 1.0°). The median value for the CRYSTALLINE class is **0.011** — representing the detection floor, denoting zero measurable amorphous content.\n',
    54: '# 8. Poisson-Calibrated Peak Detection\n\n## 8.1 Variable Poisson Noise Thresholding\n\nX-ray photon detection follows a **Poisson distribution**, where variance equals mean count:\n\n$$P(k;\\mu) = \\frac{\\mu^{k}e^{-\\mu}}{k!}, \\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(k)=\\mu \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\sigma = \\sqrt{\\mu}$$\n\nNoise magnitude scales with the square root of signal intensity ($\\sigma = \\sqrt{N}$), necessitating a dynamically scaled threshold ($k\\sqrt{\\text{counts}}$) rather than a static scalar cutoff across scans of differing dwell times.\n\n| Measurement | Counts | Expected Noise σ | Relative Noise |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Long scan | 10,000 | √10,000 = **100** | 1% |\n| Quick scan | 100 | √100 = **10** | 10% |\n\nA static threshold would suppress real reflections in quick scans while admitting noise artifacts in long scans.\n\n## 8.2 Multiple Comparisons and Bonferroni Correction\n\nApplying a naive $3\\sigma$ threshold across a 2,250-point diffractogram with $n_{\\text{eff}} \\approx 250$ independent resolution elements yields ~0.7 expected false-positive noise peaks per scan purely due to multiple testing:\n\n$$n_{\\text{eff}} = \\frac{2250\\ \\text{points}}{9\\ \\text{points/}0.2^\\circ} \\approx 250$$\n\nEnforcing a family-wise error rate $\\alpha \\le 0.05$ across the full profile, combined with the $\\sqrt{2}$ noise variance of peak prominence calculations, establishes the calibrated significance multiplier:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\;k = \\sqrt{2}\\;\\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{\\alpha}{2\\,n_{\\text{eff}}}\\right)\\;}$$\n\nEvaluating to **k ≈ 5.1–5.2** for standard scanning geometries in this facility. Threshold $k$ dynamically scales with scan length to guarantee a constant expected false-positive rate across all measurements.\n',
    56: '**Validation.** Empirical noise measurements across real scans follow the theoretical $\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\mu}$ Poisson relationship across two orders of count magnitude. Consecutive point differencing ($(\\Delta y)/\\sqrt{2}$) isolates point-to-point stochastic noise from baseline drift.\n',
    57: '### Parametric Bootstrap Validation on Synthetic Noise-Only Profiles\n\nGenerating zero-peak synthetic patterns confirms that $k=3$ detects false noise peaks, whereas the Bonferroni-derived threshold $k \\approx 5.1$ achieves exactly zero false detections.\n',
    59: '### Impact on Expert-Labeled Corpus\n\nApplying calibrated thresholding eliminates spurious peak detections in amorphous samples while preserving true peaks in crystalline samples:\n\n| Class | n | Median Peak Count, k = 3 | Median Peak Count, Calibrated k |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AMORFO | 486 | **2.0** | **0.0** |\n| INCIPIENTE | 1,011 | 15.0 | 8.0 |\n| CRISTALINO | 355 | 22.0 | **19.0** |\n\nProportion of samples attributed **three or more reflections**:\n\n| Class | k = 3 | Calibrated k |\n|---|---|---|\n| AMORFO | 0.47 | **0.11** |\n| CRISTALINO | 0.99 | **0.98** |\n\nSpurious peak attribution in amorphous samples drops from 47% to 11%, preventing noise spikes from corrupting downstream lattice descriptors (`d_max_angstrom`, `two_theta_first_peak`, `scherrer_size_nm`). `n_peaks_k3` is retained as a diagnostic reference: large divergence between `n_peaks_k3` and `n_peaks` serves as a distinctive signature of noisy amorphous patterns.\n',
    61: '# 9. Partially Crystalline Systems: Weak Bragg Reflections on Diffuse Halos\n\nApplying the calibrated pipeline to challenging `HALO_DOMINANTE` synthesis outcomes, selected reproducibly via database query based on halo-to-noise ratio.\n',
    64: 'The three cases correspond to **triazine phosphonate (LIG-C)** with distinct metal centers. Diffuse halos are quantified while weak Bragg reflections are reliably resolved above local Poisson noise floors.\n',
    65: '# 10. Comprehensive Descriptor Vector Specification\n\nDetailed code derivations for all 29 scalar descriptors across four functional categories:\n\n| Category | Count | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Traceability & Provenance** | 5 | Algorithmic parameters and pipeline version tracking |\n| **Peak Geometry** | 8 | Reflection count, positions, FWHM, and Scherrer domain sizes |\n| **Phase Composition** | 10 | Integrated crystalline and amorphous fractions |\n| **Global Profile Metrics** | 3 | Peak-to-background, spectral entropy, and global SNR |\n',
    66: '## 10.0 Visual Geometry: Spatial Mapping of Descriptors\n\nMapping the complete descriptor vector onto an annotated diffractogram of SMP-0008 (Ca + LIG-B, labeled `INCIPIENTE`).\n',
    68: '### Multi-Panel Diffractogram Annotation\n\n- **Panel (a) Full Scan Profile:** Displays low-angle window (5–15° 2θ), high-angle scaling window (>45° 2θ), scaled blank $kE$, diffuse halo $A$, and Bragg reflections.\n- **Panel (b) Reflection Broadening:** FWHM measurement for Scherrer crystallite size estimation.\n- **Panel (c) Integrated Phase Composition:** Relative area distributions of instrumental background, amorphous halo, and crystalline diffraction.\n- **Panel (d) Spectral Intensity Distribution:** Cumulative intensity concentration and spectral entropy.\n- **Panel (e) Feature Vector:** Tabulated descriptor values.\n',
    69: '### Descriptor Reference Table (29 Features)\n\n| Group | Descriptor | Unit | Visual Location | Analytical Role |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Traceability** | `pipeline_version` | — | — | Code release version |\n| | `baseline_method` | — | — | Baseline estimator type |\n| | `baseline_lambda` | — | — | arPLS stiffness parameter |\n| | `broad_width_deg` | deg | — | Sliding window width (Method 2 reference) |\n| | `peak_sigma_k` | σ | (a), legend | Applied Bonferroni-corrected Poisson multiplier |\n| | `blank_scale_factor` | — | (a), blue band | Scaling factor $k$ matching blank to scan |\n| **Peak Geometry** | `n_peaks` | count | (a), markers | Statistically verified Bragg reflections |\n| | `n_peaks_k3` | count | — | Diagnostic peak count at uncorrected 3σ |\n| | `n_peaks_below_15deg` | count | (a), gray band | Large-pore diagnostic reflection count |\n| | `two_theta_first_peak` | deg | (a), red arrow | Angular position of primary low-angle reflection |\n| | `d_max_angstrom` | Å | (a), red arrow | Maximum observed lattice d-spacing via Bragg |\n| | `d_max_saturated` | bool | — | Flags whether primary reflection hits 5.0° cutoff |\n| | `fwhm_median_deg` | deg | (b) | Median reflection FWHM |\n| | `fwhm_min_deg` | deg | (b) | Minimum reflection FWHM (upper crystalline bound) |\n| | `scherrer_size_nm` | nm | (b) | Apparent crystallite domain size |\n| **Phase Composition** | `amorphous_fraction_blank` | frac | (a) orange, (c) | Non-Bragg scattering fraction |\n| | `crystalline_fraction_blank` | frac | (c) | Complementary crystalline fraction |\n| | `crystalline_fraction` | frac | — | Legacy v2.0 estimate (§7.2) |\n| | `crystalline_area` | a.u. | (c) | Integrated Bragg diffraction area |\n| | `amorphous_area` | a.u. | (c) | Integrated diffuse scattering area |\n| | `amorphous_hump_center` | deg | (a), dashed line | Diffuse halo centroid position |\n| | `amorphous_hump_fwhm` | deg | (a) | Diffuse halo broadening |\n| | `frac_intensity_below_15deg` | frac | (a), gray band | Relative intensity in large unit-cell window |\n| | `signal_snr_area` | — | — | Integrated area signal-to-noise ratio |\n| | `crystallinity_reliable` | bool | — | Data quality reliability flag |\n| **Global Metrics** | `peak_to_background_ratio` | — | (d) | Peak contrast above instrumental floor |\n| | `spectral_entropy` | bits | (d) | Profile intensity dispersion |\n| | `snr_global` | — | (a), green arrow | Maximum net peak SNR |\n| | `raw_counts_max` | counts | (a), green arrow | Unnormalized maximum detector count |\n',
    70: '### End-to-End Computational Pipeline Flowchart\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    X([".xrdml — raw instrument scan, variable grid"])\n    S0["[0] resample_to_grid"]\n    S1["[1] normalize_by_area"]\n    S2["[2] arpls_baseline"]\n    S3a["[3a] amorphous_from_blank — A = B − kE"]\n    S3b["[3b] N = I − B — net signal"]\n    S4["[4] poisson_noise_level"]\n    S5["[5] detect_peaks_poisson"]\n    S6["[6] extract_descriptors"]\n    OUT[("pxrd_descriptors — one record per measurement")]\n\n    X --> S0\n    S0 -->|"canonical grid 5–50°, step 0.02° (2,250 points)"| S1\n    S1 -->|"total area = 1.0, scale-invariant profiles"| S2\n    S2 -->|"B, total baseline (instrument + diffuse)"| S3a\n    S2 --> S3b\n    S3b -->|"σ = √counts"| S4\n    S4 -->|"Bragg peaks exceeding k·σ"| S5\n    S3a --> S6\n    S5 --> S6\n    S6 -->|"29 descriptors: 6 provenance, 9 geometry, 10 phase, 4 global"| OUT\n\n    classDef src fill:#E3F2FD,stroke:#1E88E5,color:#0D47A1\n    classDef proc fill:#F1F8E9,stroke:#7CB342,color:#33691E\n    classDef out fill:#FFF3E0,stroke:#FB8C00,color:#E65100\n    class X src\n    class S0,S1,S2,S3a,S3b,S4,S5,S6 proc\n    class OUT out\n```\n',
    72: '## 10.1 Traceability & Provenance Descriptors (5 Features)\n\nTracks execution metadata ensuring complete auditability and backwards compatibility across database migrations.\n',
    74: "## 10.2 Peak Geometry Descriptors (8 Features)\n\n### `n_peaks` and `n_peaks_k3` — Detected Reflections\n\n```python\nk = peak_k_sigma(len(x), min_separation_points)   # ~5.1-5.2 here\npeaks, widths = detect_peaks_poisson(x, narrow, sigma, k_sigma=k)\npeaks_k3, _   = detect_peaks_poisson(x, narrow, sigma, k_sigma=3.0)\nn_peaks    = len(peaks)\nn_peaks_k3 = len(peaks_k3)\n```\n\n`n_peaks` applies the Bonferroni-corrected threshold (§8.2); all downstream peak descriptors derive from this set. `n_peaks_k3` serves as a comparison benchmark to identify noise-dominated scans.\n\n### `two_theta_first_peak` — Primary Reflection Position\n\n```python\ntwo_theta_first_peak = float(x[peaks[0]])\n```\n\n### `d_max_angstrom` and `d_max_saturated` — Maximum Lattice Spacing\n\n```python\ntheta_rad = np.radians(two_theta_first_peak / 2.0)\nd_max_angstrom = WAVELENGTH_CU_KA1 / (2.0 * np.sin(theta_rad))       # Bragg's law\nd_max_saturated = two_theta_first_peak <= LOW_ANGLE_CUTOFF_DEG + 0.10\n```\n\n`d_max_saturated` prevents models from mistaking the 5.0° instrument cutoff for intrinsic chemistry.\n\n### `fwhm_median_deg` and `fwhm_min_deg` — Reflection Broadening\n\n```python\nfwhm_median_deg = float(np.median(widths))   # representative pattern broadening\nfwhm_min_deg    = float(np.min(widths))      # sharpest reflection: upper quality bound\n```\n\n### `scherrer_size_nm` — Apparent Crystallite Domain Size\n\n```python\nbeta_rad = np.radians(float(widths[0]))\nscherrer_size_nm = (0.9 * (WAVELENGTH_CU_KA1 * 0.1)) / (beta_rad * np.cos(theta_rad))\n```\n\nApplies Scherrer equation ($K=0.9$, shape factor for FWHM). Serves as a **comparative index** within identical instrument setups, bounded by `reference_standard` resolution.\n",
    76: '## 10.3 Phase Composition Descriptors (10 Features)\n\n### `crystalline_area`, `amorphous_area`, `crystalline_fraction`\n\n```python\ncrystalline_area     = integrate(crystalline, x)    # trapezoidal integration\namorphous_area       = integrate(halo, x)\ncrystalline_fraction = crystalline_area / (crystalline_area + amorphous_area)\n```\n\nBoth integrated areas are stored alongside the normalized fraction to preserve absolute scattering scale.\n\n### `signal_snr_area` and `crystallinity_reliable`\n\n```python\nangular_span    = float(x[-1] - x[0])\nsignal_snr_area = (crystalline_area + amorphous_area) / (sigma * angular_span)\ncrystallinity_reliable = signal_snr_area >= MIN_SIGNAL_SNR_AREA\n```\n\nEmpirical reference values: blank ~0.07, standard scan 3–5.\n\n### `amorphous_hump_center` — Halo Centroid\n\n```python\namorphous_hump_center = integrate(x * halo, x) / integrate(halo, x)\n```\n\nBoth numerator and denominator are definite integrals over degrees, canceling step size $\\Delta\\theta$. Indicates average intermolecular contact distance (e.g., 20–25° 2θ corresponds to 3.5–4.5 Å).\n\n### `amorphous_hump_fwhm` — Halo Broadening\n\n```python\npeak_halo = halo.max()\nabove_half = np.where(halo >= peak_halo / 2.0)[0]\namorphous_hump_fwhm = float(x[above_half[-1]] - x[above_half[0]])\n```\n\n### `frac_intensity_below_15deg` — Large Unit Cell Scattering Fraction\n\n```python\nwindow = x < LOW_ANGLE_WINDOW_DEG\nfrac_intensity_below_15deg = integrate(normalized[window], x[window])\n```\n\nPeak-detection independent metric quantifying low-angle scattering weight.\n\n### `peak_to_background_ratio` — Profile Contrast\n\n```python\nbackground_level = float(np.median(instrumental))\npeak_to_background_ratio = float(np.max(crystalline) / background_level)\n```\n\n### `amorphous_fraction_blank`, `crystalline_fraction_blank`, `blank_scale_factor`\n\n```python\nwindow = x >= BLANK_SCALE_FROM_DEG                      # default 45° 2θ\nscale  = np.median(base[window]) / np.median(blank[window])\nblank_scale_factor = scale\n\ninstrument = scale * blank\namorphous  = np.maximum(base - instrument, 0.0)\namorphous_fraction_blank = integrate(amorphous, x) / integrate(normalized, x)\ncrystalline_fraction_blank = 1.0 - amorphous_fraction_blank\n```\n\nCalculates non-Bragg scattering fraction normalized to total profile area.\n',
    78: '## 10.4 Global Profile Descriptors (3 Features)\n\nValid across fully amorphous samples without requiring peak detection.\n\n### `spectral_entropy` — Intensity Dispersion\n\n```python\np = net / net.sum()\np = p[p > 0]\nspectral_entropy = float(-np.sum(p * np.log2(p)))\n```\n\nShannon entropy ($H = -\\sum p_i \\log_2 p_i$). Crystalline profiles with sharp peaks yield **low entropy**; amorphous diffuse halos yield **high entropy**. Correlates strongly with crystallinity ($r = -0.73$). Calculated strictly on **net signal** to prevent baseline noise from masking signal entropy.\n\n### `snr_global` — Net Peak-to-Noise Ratio\n\n```python\nsnr_global = float(np.max(net) / sigma)\n```\n\n### `raw_counts_max` — Unnormalized Maximum Intensity\n\n```python\nraw_counts_max = float(np.max(y_raw))\n```\n\nIndexes acquisition dwell time and specimen mass.\n',
    81: '### Theoretical Entropy Limits ($\\log_2 N$) and Effective Angular Width\n\nOn a 2,250-point grid, theoretical maximum entropy is $\\log_2 2250 = 11.14$ bits. A crystalline scan with ~40 reflections (FWHM 0.2°) spanning 17.8% of the axis evaluates to $\\log_2(0.178 \\times 2250) = 8.64$ bits (measured corpus median: 8.46 bits).\n\nTransforming bits to **effective angular width** ($2^H \\cdot \\Delta\\theta$):\n- **CISTALINO:** 8.46 bits $\\rightarrow$ **7.1°**\n- **INCIPIENTE:** 8.94 bits $\\rightarrow$ 9.8°\n- **AMORFO:** 9.24 bits $\\rightarrow$ **12.1°**\n\nCrystalline intensity concentrates within 7.1° of the 45° scan range, whereas amorphous scattering disperses across 12.1°.\n',
    83: '# 11. Pipeline Verification & Quality Assurance\n\nValidating pipeline outputs across four independent testing suites: external benchmark controls, mathematical hand-calculation checks, physical boundary validation, and internal area balance.\n',
    85: '**Empirical Benchmark Checks:**\n- **Blank Control:** Empty holder yields $X_c \\approx 0.02$, $\\text{SNR}_{\\text{area}} \\approx 0.07$, flagged as `crystallinity_reliable = FALSE`.\n- **Precursor Salts:** Well-ordered nitrate salts (Mg, Cu, Ni, Ca, Zn, Bi) yield $X_c = 0.91\\text{--}0.96$.\n- **Free Linkers:** Triazine and pyrene phosphonates yield $X_c = 0.57\\text{--}0.71$, characteristic of organic molecular crystals.\n- **Instrument Standard:** Certified Si standard defines minimum instrument FWHM floor.\n',
    87: '## 11.5 Reference Ground-Truth Matrix\n\n| Reference Sample | Physical Class | Expected Output |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Empty Sample Holder** | Zero solid sample | $A \\approx 0$: zero coherent diffraction or sample diffuse scattering |\n| **NIST 640 Si Standard** | Certified crystalline standard | Low $A$, reflecting substrate contribution (§7.5) |\n| **Labeled Amorphous Sample** | Glassy disordered solid | Elevated $A$ |\n',
    89: '# 12. Database Ingestion Architecture (`pxrd_descriptors`)\n\n## 12.1 Schema Separation: Algorithmic Descriptors vs. Human Annotations\n\n- **`pxrd_descriptors`**: Algorithmic pipeline outputs, recomputable on version migrations.\n- **`pxrd_labels`**: High-value human expert annotations, preserved across recalculations.\n\nLinked by `measurement_id` (UUID) with `ON DELETE CASCADE` referencing `characterization_measurements`.\n',
    91: '## 12.2 Batch UPSERT Execution Protocol\n\nIngestion utilizes PostgreSQL `ON CONFLICT (measurement_id) DO UPDATE` to update existing records idempotently while tracking `pipeline_version`.\n',
    94: '# 13. Exploratory Corpus Analysis\n\nExecuting SQL analytics across the populated descriptor database to examine corpus-wide distributions and inter-feature correlations.\n',
    97: '**Corpus Findings.**\n- **(a)** Calculated crystallinity spans a broad continuous range, confirming discriminant capacity.\n- **(b)** Spectral entropy (plotted as effective angular width in degrees) correlates negatively with crystallinity ($r = -0.70$).\n- **(c)** Maximum lattice d-spacing and low-angle reflection count exhibit mutual consistency.\n',
    99: '# 14. Methodological Best Practices & Engineering Guidelines\n\n1. **An unexecuted notebook is a proposal, not a validated result.** Ensure complete execution runs without errors.\n2. **Calibrate free parameters against known ground-truth controls.**\n3. **Always evaluate blank controls.** Pipeline outputs on empty holders expose false-positive signal generation.\n4. **Beware of silent algorithmic failures.** Floor-estimating algorithms (arPLS) return zero envelopes without throwing exceptions.\n5. **Enforce physical boundary assertions** (angles $\\le 180^\\circ$, fractions $\\le 1.0$, non-negative dimensions).\n6. **Maintain dimensional consistency between sums and integrals.**\n7. **Algebraically simplify formulas before implementation** to avoid redundant collinear features.\n8. **Execution order matters**: area normalization must precede baseline fitting.\n9. **Distinguish unmeasured regions from zero signal** via boolean validity masks.\n10. **Store computational provenance alongside data** (`pipeline_version`, hyperparameters).\n11. **Encode measurement caveats as explicit flags** (`d_max_saturated`, `crystallinity_reliable`).\n12. **Quantitatively verify algorithmic assumptions** on experimental distributions.\n13. **Validate bug fixes against baseline controls** to prevent regressions on valid cases.\n14. **High correlation ranking does not imply absolute quantitative accuracy.**\n15. **Select benchmark examples via objective statistical queries** rather than subjective visual cherry-picking.\n',
    100: '# Conclusions & Next Steps\n\n**Database State:** `pxrd_descriptors` contains validated descriptor vectors for all analytical scans, tracked in `data_dictionary` and generated via `src/pxrd_descriptors.py` with full parameter provenance.\n\n**Analytical Boundaries:**\n- Crystallinity descriptors provide continuous rankings; phase identification resides in `pxrd_labels`.\n- `d_max_angstrom` is bounded by instrument optics at 17.66 Å.\n- Scherrer calculations provide relative domain indices, bounded by instrument broadening.\n\n## Non-Negative Matrix Factorization (NMF) Integration\n\nNMF decomposes the full $N \\times 2250$ net signal matrix into constituent component phase patterns. Reference scans calibrate baseline filters, while NMF extracts latent phases directly from the experimental corpus.\n\n## Subsequent Milestones\n\n1. Execute full batch processing across all ~1,880 scans.\n2. Expand expert human annotations (`pxrd_labels`).\n3. Apply NMF phase decomposition to net signal arrays.\n4. Execute automated novelty triage comparing peak positions against simulated single-crystal SCXRD patterns.\n',
}
